'''accuse†''' (a̤-kūz′), ''n.'' [=It. ''accusa'', charge; from the verb.] Accusation.
{{smaller block|<poem>York . . .
By false ''accuse'' doth level at my life.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., iii. 1.}}}}

'''accusement†''' (a̤-kūz′mẹnt), ''n.'' [< ME. ''acusement'', < OF. *''acusement'', ''accusement'', < ''acuser'', accuse.] Accusation.
{{smaller block|By forged ''accusements'' . . . were condemned.
{{right|''Holinshed.''}}}}

'''accuser''' (a̤-kū′zėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''accuser'', ''accusour'', < AF. ''accusour'', OF. ''acusor'', ''acuseor'', F. ''accusateur'', < L. ''accusator'', accuser, < ''accusare'': see ''accuse'', ''v.''] One who accuses or blames; specifically, a person who formally accuses another of an offense before a magistrate or a tribunal of any kind.

'''accusingly''' (a̤-kū′zing-li), ''adv.'' In an accusing manner.

'''accustom''' (a̤-kus′to̤m), ''v.'' [< late ME. ''acustome'', ''acustume'', < OF. ''acoustumer'', ''acostumer'' (F. ''accoutumer'' = Sp. ''acostumbrar'' = Pg. ''acostumar'' = It. ''accostumare''), < ''a'' (L. ''ad''), to, + ''coustume'' (F. ''coutume''), custom: see ''custom''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To familiarize by custom or use; habituate or inure: as, to ''accustom'' one’s self to a spare diet; time may ''accustom'' one to almost anything; to be ''accustomed'' to hard work.
{{smaller block|So ''accustomed'' to his freaks and follies, that she viewed them all as matters of course.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Twice-Told Tales, I. 176.}}}}
{{smaller block|We are not ''accustomed'' to express our thoughts or emotions by symbolical actions. {{right|''Emerson'', Misc., p. 24.}}}}
{{smaller|=Syn. To habituate, familiarize, inure, harden, train.}}

'''II.'''† ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To be wont or habituated to

