'''accustomate†''' (a-kus′tọm-āt), ''a.'' [= OF. ''acostomé'' = It. ''accostumato'' = Pg. ''acostumado'' = Sp. ''acostumbrado'' (in adv. ''acostumbradamente''); ''accustom'' + ''-ate''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''accustomed.''] Customary. ''Card. Bainbridge.''

'''accustomed''' (a-kus′tọmd), ''p. a.'' [< ME. ''acustomed''; pp. of ''accustom.''] 1. Often practised or used; customary; habitual; made familiar through use; usual; wonted: as, in their ''accustomed'' manner.
{{smaller block|It is an ''accustomed'' action with her. ''Shak.'', Macb., v. 1.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>My old ''accustomed'' corner here is,
The table still is in the nook;
Ah! vanished many a busy year is
This well-known chair since last I took.</poem>
{{right|''Thackeray'', Ballad of Bouillabaisse.}}}}
2†. Having custom or patronage; frequented.
{{smaller block|A well-''accustom’d'' house, a handsome barkeeper, with clean obliging drawers, soon get the master an estate.
{{right|''Mrs. Centlivre'', Bold Stroke, i. 1.}}}}

'''accustomedness''' (a-kus′tọmd-nes), ''n.'' Familiarity; wontedness; the quality of being accustomed (to). [Rare.]
{{smaller block|''Accustomedness'' to sin hardens the heart.
{{right|''Bp. Pearce'', Sermons, p. 230.}}}}
{{smaller block|Freedom from that bad ''accustomedness'' to evil and wrong. {{right|''The American'', VII. 164.}}}}

